  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1049 PM MDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\nWINDY WEDNESDAY:\n\nA strong and cold trough is expected to track across the Great \nBasin tonight, approaching eastern Utah and western Colorado by \ndaybreak. There isn't expected to be much moisture with this \nsystem, at least at the surface, meaning any showers that \ndevelop will be isolated and more likely to produce gusty winds \nthan wetting rain. What this system will have plenty of is wind.\nThe surface pressure gradient will tighten up substantially \nahead of the approaching cold front, and mixing will tap into \nthe 100-110 knot jet aloft. As a result, widespread gusts of \n45-55 mph are expected, with some higher winds possible across \nthe higher elevations. Wind Advisories remain in effect for all \nvalley zones in eastern Utah and western Colorado. In addition, \ndry surface conditions combined with the high winds will result \nin elevated fire weather concerns across the region, though only\nportions of northwest Colorado have critical fuels. \n\nThe cold front sweeps through tomorrow afternoon and evening, \nushering in cooler air. Temperatures tomorrow night into \nThursday will run near-normal for late April. Light rain and \nsnow showers will be possible across the Park Range and eastern \nUintas through Thursday, as post-frontal northwesterly flow \ntakes advantage of favorable orographics.\n\nCOOLER AND UNSETTLED PATTERN CONTINUES:\n\nWinds remain breezy through the week and into the weekend, as a\n75- 85 knot jet remains in place over the Four Corners region. \nDaytime mixing will bring down afternoon wind gusts of 25-35 \nmph, in some locations up to 40 mph. Modest warm air advection \nFriday and Saturday will allow temperatures to rebound to around\n5 degrees above normal. However, another cool and potent trough\nis expected to set up Sunday into early next week. Current \nforecast PWATs with this system are in the 150-200% of normal \nrange, meaning there is a chance of wetting valley rain and \naccumulating mountain snow. However, confidence in this remains \nlow (<40%). Regardless of the chances of precipitation, there is\na high likelihood (>80%) that temperatures will once again drop\nbelow normal for the time of year. There is also the potential \nfor freeze concerns across the lower elevation valleys early \nnext week. Wild spring weather continues apace.",
